From 6e4d5e970c8f4f2988d7f32b56f51ced142e058c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 08:08:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Create a buffer-export Vulkan device when the board has no
 Vulkan Video

The compositor path presents the decoder's DMA-BUF through wl_dmabuf and
never records a GPU command, but the native frame producer still validates
the adopted Vulkan handles before it can export a frame. On the STM32MP25
the VeriSilicon device has no VK_KHR_video_queue or decode extensions, so
the shared-device probe rejected every candidate and the interface was left
without a device: the stream window reported "The streamer has no Vulkan
device for the video" and presented nothing.

A board that decodes through V4L2 Request or VA-API does not need a Vulkan
Video decoder. Build the shared device as a plain buffer-export device in
that case: take the first graphics queue family instead of reserving an
isolated Qt queue, advertise no decode profiles, and keep the instance and
swapchain extensions the interface needs to adopt the device. The gate in
ApplicationStartup no longer probes for Vulkan Video first, so the device is
created on these boards instead of being skipped.

Qt only wraps the native instance in a QVulkanInstance when the scene graph
draws with Vulkan. With OpenGL the streamer keeps the device to itself, so
Qt is no longer asked to take over an instance it never uses.

Ask FFmpeg only for the surface extensions this session has, which also
removes its "extension not found, excluding" warnings on a Wayland-only
board.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [STM32MP25 performance work]
---
